 otherwise, return False.",798,,"[""Here's a Python function that uses the `xml.etree.ElementTree` module to parse the XML document and check for the required elements and formats:

```python
import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET
from dateutil.parser import isoparse

def validate_atom(xml_string):
    root = ET.fromstring(xml_string)

    if not (root.tag == '{http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom}feed' or 
            root.tag == '{http://purl.org/atom/ns#}feed'):
        return False

    found_title = False
    found_id = False
    found_link = False
    found_published = False

    for child in root:
        if child.tag == '{http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom}entry':
            for elem in child:
                if elem.tag == '{http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom}title' and len(elem.text) > 0:
                    found_title = True
                elif elem.tag == '{http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom}id':
                    found_id = True
                elif elem.tag == '{http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom}link':
                    if not elem.attrib.get('href'):
                        return False
                    else:
                        found_link = True
                elif elem.tag == '{http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom}published':
                    try:
                        isoparse(elem.text)
                        found_published = True
                    except ValueError:
                        return False

    if not all([found_title, found_id, found_link, found_published]):
        return False

    return True
```

This function parses the XML string into an `ElementTree` object. It then checks each element in the tree to see if it matches one of the required elements and whether they are present or have a valid format. If all conditions are met, the function returns `True`

